Chapter: PciBridgeCapUnder
  grounds Device.Port
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 The depth cap, and the branch that refuses. pci-scan-max-depth is 3 and
 pci-collect's `depth > max` arm had never executed anywhere, because no bed
 had ever presented a fourth level. A fuel cap that has never been shown to
 say no is an assertion rather than a cap (L-FALSIF).

 This is the UNDER-THE-CAP control for pci-bridge-cap: three levels, so the
 walk reaches bus 3 and stops because there is nothing deeper, not because it
 was refused. Without it, bus4=0 in the four-level arm would be indis-
 tinguishable from a walk that simply ran out of topology.

Section: Body
  count-on-bus : List PciDevice, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  count-on-bus (devs) (i) (n) (bus) (acc) =
   if i >= n then acc
   else if (list-at devs i).pci-bus == bus then count-on-bus devs (i + 1) n bus (acc + 1)
   else count-on-bus devs (i + 1) n bus acc

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   let scan = pci-scan-all
   in let n = scan.count
   in let b1 = count-on-bus (scan.devices) 0 n 1 0
   in let b2 = count-on-bus (scan.devices) 0 n 2 0
   in let b3 = count-on-bus (scan.devices) 0 n 3 0
   in let b4 = count-on-bus (scan.devices) 0 n 4 0
   in print-line-uni ("count=" & show n & " bus1=" & show b1 & " bus2=" & show b2 & " bus3=" & show b3 & " bus4=" & show b4 & " truncated=" & (if scan.truncated then "yes" else "no"))
  end